import PolicyLayout from '../../layouts/PolicyLayout.astro';
---
<PolicyLayout
  appName="PassionPlay"
  icon="🔥"
  title="PassionPlay — Privacy Policy"
  description="Effective date: August 13, 2026"
>
  <div class="card summary-card" slot="meta">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your game stays on your device.</strong> Player names and progress are saved on your phone and are never sent to us or to anyone else.</li>
      <li>The card decks ship inside the app — setting up and playing a game needs no internet connection at all.</li>
      <li>The app is <strong>18+</strong> and opens only after a one-time age confirmation, which is remembered on your device.</li>
      <li>The app is free, funded by full-screen ads (Google AdMob) that appear between cards every few draws.</li>
      <li><strong>The app asks Google for non-personalized ads.</strong> Serving them still involves a third-party SDK that accesses advertising identifiers, as described in Section 4.</li>
      <li>Uninstalling the app deletes everything it has stored.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Who this policy covers</h2>
  <p>
    PassionPlay ("the app", "we", "our") is a pass-and-play card game for couples on Android and
    iOS. Two or more players take turns drawing cards across three stages, passing one phone
    between them. This policy explains what information the app stores on your device, what a
    third-party advertising SDK accesses on our behalf, and your choices. The app has no server or
    backend of its own — the only outside party involved is Google, through the AdMob SDK described
    in Section 4.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Information the app accesses</h2>
  <p>
    Everything below is created by you when you set up and play a game, and is written to
    app-private storage on your device. None of it is transmitted to us or to any third party — the
    app itself makes no network connections whatsoever, and the only traffic it generates is the ad
    requests described in Section 4, which carry nothing from this table.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Data</th><th>Why it's collected</th><th>Where it's stored</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Player names</td>
        <td>Entered during setup and displayed during play so it's clear whose turn it is.</td>
        <td>Saved on your device alongside the rest of the game, so you can close the app and resume the same session later.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Game progress</td>
        <td>Which stage you're on, how far into it you are, the shuffled order of that stage's cards, and whose turn comes next — everything needed to resume where you left off.</td>
        <td>The same local save as above. Cleared automatically when a game finishes, or when you discard it from the setup screen.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Language choice</td>
        <td>Remembered so the app opens in the language you picked instead of asking on every launch.</td>
        <td>Local device storage on your device only.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>18+ confirmation</td>
        <td>Whether you have already confirmed you are 18 or older, so the age screen isn't shown again every launch.</td>
        <td>Local device storage on your device only.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ad counter</td>
        <td>A count of how many cards have been revealed since the last ad, used to decide when the next one appears.</td>
        <td>Stored with the local game save; see Section 4.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Card decks and app text</td>
        <td>The questions, dares, and interface wording in both supported languages.</td>
        <td>Bundled into the app install itself — this is app content, not user data, and it never changes based on anything you do.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>3. Device permissions we ask for</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Permission</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Internet</td>
        <td>Used only to request and display ads. Setting up a game, drawing cards, resuming a saved game, and everything else the app does work with no connection at all.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Vibration</td>
        <td>A brief haptic tap when a card is revealed.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Advertising ID</td>
        <td>Accessed by the Google AdMob SDK to request and measure ads — see Section 4.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="muted">
    You can grant or revoke these from your device's system settings. The app never asks for camera,
    microphone, location, contacts, or access to your photos. Its Android build additionally declares
    a screen-overlay permission and two legacy external-storage permissions that come from the React
    Native framework the app is built on; PassionPlay never exercises them and reads or writes
    nothing outside its own private storage.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Advertising &amp; third-party SDKs</h2>
  <p>
    PassionPlay is free and funded by advertising rather than a purchase price or a subscription.
    Ads are full-screen interstitials shown between cards, roughly every five to eight card reveals.
    They are not tied to any reward, coin, or currency, and dismissing one returns you straight to
    the game — there is nothing in the app you can only unlock by watching an ad.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ads are served by <strong>Google AdMob</strong>, a third-party advertising SDK we embed but
    don't control. <strong>The app requests non-personalized ads only</strong>, which tells Google
    not to use an advertising profile to target what you are shown. Even so, to request, deliver,
    and measure ads at all, Google's SDK can access and process:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your device's advertising identifier.</li>
    <li>An approximate location derived from your IP address — the app itself requests no location permission and has no access to your precise location.</li>
    <li>Signals relating to ad requests and impressions, and diagnostic information about ad delivery.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of this ad-related data is collected, stored, or seen by us directly — it is handled
    entirely inside Google's SDK under Google's own privacy policy, available at
    <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">policies.google.com/privacy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    On Android, you can limit how this identifier is used at any time from your device's system
    settings, under <em>Settings → Privacy → Ads</em>, where Android lets you reset your advertising
    ID or delete it entirely. On iOS, the app does not ask for permission to track you, and iOS
    withholds the advertising identifier from any app that has not asked — so no iOS advertising
    identifier is available to the SDK.
  </p>
  <p>
    Aside from AdMob, the app uses no other third-party SDKs, no analytics, and no crash-reporting
    service. <strong>Your player names, game progress, language choice, and age confirmation are
    never sent to Google or to anyone else</strong> — that data stays on your device as described in
    Section 2.
  </p>

  <h2>5. What we don't do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We don't require an account, sign-in, or email address to use the app.</li>
    <li>We don't operate a server, so there is nowhere for your game data to be uploaded to.</li>
    <li>We don't collect analytics, crash reports, or usage statistics of our own.</li>
    <li>We don't sell, rent, or share your player names or game progress — we have never had access to them.</li>
    <li>We don't record which cards you answered and which you declined; nothing about how you play leaves the position needed to resume.</li>
    <li>We don't request location, contacts, camera, microphone, or access to your photos.</li>
    <li>We don't include an in-app browser or load remote content beyond the ads described in Section 4.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Mature content &amp; age gate</h2>
  <p>
    PassionPlay is an adult game intended only for players aged 18 or older. Its cards are arranged
    into three stages — Hardtalk, Prelude, and Hardcore — each more explicit than the one before,
    covering intimate questions and dares written for couples. Before the app can be used at all it
    shows a one-time screen asking you to confirm you are 18 or older; answering that you are under
    18 blocks access, and the confirmation is remembered on your device as described in Section 2 so
    it isn't asked again.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cards also display a penalty measured in shots, for players who would rather decline than answer.
    The app enforces nothing and records nothing about this: declining any card is always allowed,
    with or without drinking, and the app's own rules screen says so alongside a note on drinking
    responsibly.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Data retention &amp; deletion</h2>
  <p>
    Everything the app stores lives on your device only, for as long as the app is installed. You can remove it at any time by:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Finishing a game, which clears that game's save automatically, or</li>
    <li>Choosing "Start a new game" on the setup screen, which discards a game in progress after asking you to confirm, or</li>
    <li>Using your device's app-storage / clear-data option, or</li>
    <li>Uninstalling the app, which removes every saved game, your language choice, and your age confirmation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We hold no account and no server-side data about you, so there is nothing for you to request
    deletion of from us and no process for doing so — the controls above are the complete set.
  </p>
  <p>
    One note specific to Android: the app allows the operating system's standard automatic backup, so
    a saved game and your preferences may be included in a device backup held in your own Google
    account, under your own Google settings. That backup belongs to you, not to us, and we have no
    access to it.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Security</h2>
  <p>
    Because all data stays within your device's app-sandboxed storage, it is protected by your
    operating system's standard app-sandboxing and, where enabled, device-level encryption. The app
    stores no passwords, payment details, or contact information — the most sensitive thing it holds
    is the player names you type in.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worth stating plainly rather than leaving you to assume otherwise: the saved game is ordinary
    unencrypted data inside the app's private storage. That is enough to keep it away from other apps
    on the device, but someone with root access to your phone, or with an extracted device backup,
    could read the player names and how far through a game you had gone. As with any app, we
    recommend keeping your device's operating system up to date and using a device passcode.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If PassionPlay's data practices change in the future — for example if a new online feature,
    analytics service, or additional third-party SDK is added, or if the app begins asking for iOS
    tracking permission — this policy will be updated and the effective date above will change
    accordingly. Continued use of the app after an update constitutes acceptance of the revised
    policy.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Contact us</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy or the app's data practices can be sent to
    <a href="mailto:avtukalaz@gmail.com">avtukalaz@gmail.com</a>.
  </p>

